{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"paced-persistence-serialization-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paced persistence serialization review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed product commit: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"632e46944241e64d91f46fd874de90d70d226cf8"},{"type":"text","value":".\nBase: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"9e8ed997885fb46ac98ec85906f4ca4f662e7cce"},{"type":"text","value":". The review record and\nchangeset follow that product commit. The primary executable owner is\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/paced-mutations-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"contract-and-result","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract and result"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The paced mutations guide promises at most one pending and one persisting\ntransaction for debounce and throttle. The queue strategy promises serial\npersistence. Issue #2058 demonstrated two concurrent backend handlers for\ndebounce and throttle. A manual rollback exposed the same overlap in queue:\nthe public receipt rejected before its active handler returned, so the next\nhandler started early."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repair separates admission from persistence. A rejected paced call owns an\nisolated transaction, while admitted calls can merge into the one pending\ntransaction. Debounce and throttle reserve a leading edge before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncan reenter. A shared serial pacer waits for actual "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transaction.commit()"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncompletion, rather than receipt settlement, before it starts another handler.\nQueue uses that same completion boundary. A manual rollback still rejects its\npublic receipt immediately."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"law-histories-and-observation-cuts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Law, histories, and observation cuts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The independent two-call rule starts the successor only after both its timer\nedge and the first handler's release. The oracle enumerates 24 successful\nhold/release histories across debounce and throttle. It crosses leading,\nnon-leading, and omitted-leading options, with release before, at, and after\nthe edge. The model computes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"max(eligibleAt, releaseAt)"},{"type":"text","value":" without importing\nthe production scheduler. The immediate handoff is the chosen pacing policy;\nthe guide's serialization promise alone supplies the lower bound."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The production driver uses real Collections, strategies, and transactions.\nIt controls handler promises and a virtual clock. At admission, the timer\nedge, handler release, and final drain, it checks every handler start, active\nhandler count, transaction state, receipt outcome and identity, and optimistic\nrows. Adjacent histories distinguish a renewed debounce quiet period, throttle\nspacing from actual starts, and dropped-call admission isolation. The\nsettlement grammar crosses both factories, leading modes, same/distinct keys,\nfirst-handler success or rejection, and pending cancellation. Manual rollback\ncrosses debounce, throttle, and queue. Nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":" checks one merged\nleading write, no stale timer, and a later usable admission."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Original production fails 18 of the 24 held-edge histories at the concurrency\nassertion. The six release-before-edge controls pass. Before the final repair,\nmanual rollback fails at the active-handler assertion for each of the three\nstrategies. Nested admission fails for debounce and throttle. These are\nassertion kills at the promised intermediate cuts, not timeouts. The repaired\nowner and ordinary pacing tests pass 136/136 on the reviewed base. Focused\nTypeScript, ESLint, Prettier, and the DB package build pass. The build reports\nnon-fatal missing Expo example configuration warnings."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"oracle-guide-audit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle guide audit"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result for this owner"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guide supplies serialization and timing authority. The opening oracle prose and coverage map state finite limits."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expected times come from timer eligibility and handler release, not a production pacer or classifier."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Opening prose states the contract; nearby model, action grammar, production driver, and assertions remain distinct."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 24-cell matrix is bounded enumeration with explicit release offsets. It does not claim random reach."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real production entry points reach held handlers. The recorder retains ordered starts and intermediate receipts."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Original overlap, early release after rollback, and duplicate leading starts fail their intended assertions."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No important random property is introduced. Named finite tests provide direct replay."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two-call expected rule is a prerequisite calculation, not a copied production state machine."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pendingIds"},{"type":"text","value":" combines optimistic mutations. Handler completion and public receipt settlement stay separate."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup releases held promises and preserves a primary assertion when cleanup also fails."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A second equivalent formulation is unnecessary for the max-prerequisite rule. The issue's direct two-call probe provides a separate public concurrency observation."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record states the bounded closure, kills, checks, limits, and each guide outcome for the reviewed product commit."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edge-before/at/after releases, renewed quiet, delayed actual-start spacing, and nested admission reject nearby wrong designs."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The claim is limited to controlled DB source execution. No real-server, framework, or npm handoff is claimed."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repaired source paths have no known reachable counterexample in the named\ngrammar. The coverage map owns provider echo, cleanup during a held handler,\nlonger arbitrary bursts, held default options, and multiple-manager histories.\nThose gaps limit the evidence; a passing finite suite does not prove every\npossible history. Server ordering, framework scheduling, and published npm\nbehavior require separate receiving witnesses."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"paced-persistence-serialization-review","text":"Paced persistence serialization review","level":1},{"id":"contract-and-result","text":"Contract and result","level":2},{"id":"law-histories-and-observation-cuts","text":"Law, histories, and observation cuts","level":2},{"id":"oracle-guide-audit","text":"Oracle guide audit","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Paced persistence serialization review\n\nReviewed product commit: `632e46944241e64d91f46fd874de90d70d226cf8`.\nBase: `9e8ed997885fb46ac98ec85906f4ca4f662e7cce`. The review record and\nchangeset follow that product commit. The primary executable owner is\n`packages/db/tests/paced-mutations-oracle.test.ts`.\n\n## Contract and result\n\nThe paced mutations guide promises at most one pending and one persisting\ntransaction for debounce and throttle. The queue strategy promises serial\npersistence. Issue #2058 demonstrated two concurrent backend handlers for\ndebounce and throttle. A manual rollback exposed the same overlap in queue:\nthe public receipt rejected before its active handler returned, so the next\nhandler started early.\n\nThe repair separates admission from persistence. A rejected paced call owns an\nisolated transaction, while admitted calls can merge into the one pending\ntransaction. Debounce and throttle reserve a leading edge before `onMutate`\ncan reenter. A shared serial pacer waits for actual `Transaction.commit()`\ncompletion, rather than receipt settlement, before it starts another handler.\nQueue uses that same completion boundary. A manual rollback still rejects its\npublic receipt immediately.\n\n## Law, histories, and observation cuts\n\nThe independent two-call rule starts the successor only after both its timer\nedge and the first handler's release. The oracle enumerates 24 successful\nhold/release histories across debounce and throttle. It crosses leading,\nnon-leading, and omitted-leading options, with release before, at, and after\nthe edge. The model computes `max(eligibleAt, releaseAt)` without importing\nthe production scheduler. The immediate handoff is the chosen pacing policy;\nthe guide's serialization promise alone supplies the lower bound.\n\nThe production driver uses real Collections, strategies, and transactions.\nIt controls handler promises and a virtual clock. At admission, the timer\nedge, handler release, and final drain, it checks every handler start, active\nhandler count, transaction state, receipt outcome and identity, and optimistic\nrows. Adjacent histories distinguish a renewed debounce quiet period, throttle\nspacing from actual starts, and dropped-call admission isolation. The\nsettlement grammar crosses both factories, leading modes, same/distinct keys,\nfirst-handler success or rejection, and pending cancellation. Manual rollback\ncrosses debounce, throttle, and queue. Nested `onMutate` checks one merged\nleading write, no stale timer, and a later usable admission.\n\nOriginal production fails 18 of the 24 held-edge histories at the concurrency\nassertion. The six release-before-edge controls pass. Before the final repair,\nmanual rollback fails at the active-handler assertion for each of the three\nstrategies. Nested admission fails for debounce and throttle. These are\nassertion kills at the promised intermediate cuts, not timeouts. The repaired\nowner and ordinary pacing tests pass 136/136 on the reviewed base. Focused\nTypeScript, ESLint, Prettier, and the DB package build pass. The build reports\nnon-fatal missing Expo example configuration warnings.\n\n## Oracle guide audit\n\n| Requirement | Result for this owner |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The guide supplies serialization and timing authority. The opening oracle prose and coverage map state finite limits. |\n| ORC-002 | Expected times come from timer eligibility and handler release, not a production pacer or classifier. |\n| ORC-003 | Opening prose states the contract; nearby model, action grammar, production driver, and assertions remain distinct. |\n| ORC-004 | The 24-cell matrix is bounded enumeration with explicit release offsets. It does not claim random reach. |\n| ORC-005 | Real production entry points reach held handlers. The recorder retains ordered starts and intermediate receipts. |\n| ORC-006 | Original overlap, early release after rollback, and duplicate leading starts fail their intended assertions. |\n| ORC-007 | No important random property is introduced. Named finite tests provide direct replay. |\n| ORC-008 | The two-call expected rule is a prerequisite calculation, not a copied production state machine. |\n| ORC-009 | Model `pendingIds` combines optimistic mutations. Handler completion and public receipt settlement stay separate. |\n| ORC-010 | Cleanup releases held promises and preserves a primary assertion when cleanup also fails. |\n| ORC-011 | A second equivalent formulation is unnecessary for the max-prerequisite rule. The issue's direct two-call probe provides a separate public concurrency observation. |\n| ORC-012 | This record states the bounded closure, kills, checks, limits, and each guide outcome for the reviewed product commit. |\n| ORC-013 | Edge-before/at/after releases, renewed quiet, delayed actual-start spacing, and nested admission reject nearby wrong designs. |\n| ORC-014 | The claim is limited to controlled DB source execution. No real-server, framework, or npm handoff is claimed. |\n\nThe repaired source paths have no known reachable counterexample in the named\ngrammar. The coverage map owns provider echo, cleanup during a held handler,\nlonger arbitrary bursts, held default options, and multiple-manager histories.\nThose gaps limit the evidence; a passing finite suite does not prove every\npossible history. Server ordering, framework scheduling, and published npm\nbehavior require separate receiving witnesses.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Paced persistence serialization review\n\nReviewed product commit: `632e46944241e64d91f46fd874de90d70d226cf8`.\nBase: `9e8ed997885fb46ac98ec85906f4ca4f662e7cce`. The review record and\nchangeset follow that product commit. The primary executable owner is\n`packages/db/tests/paced-mutations-oracle.test.ts`.\n\n## Contract and result\n\nThe paced mutations guide promises at most one pending and one persisting\ntransaction for debounce and throttle. The queue strategy promises serial\npersistence. Issue #2058 demonstrated two concurrent backend handlers for\ndebounce and throttle. A manual rollback exposed the same overlap in queue:\nthe public receipt rejected before its active handler returned, so the next\nhandler started early.\n\nThe repair separates admission from persistence. A rejected paced call owns an\nisolated transaction, while admitted calls can merge into the one pending\ntransaction. Debounce and throttle reserve a leading edge before `onMutate`\ncan reenter. A shared serial pacer waits for actual `Transaction.commit()`\ncompletion, rather than receipt settlement, before it starts another handler.\nQueue uses that same completion boundary. A manual rollback still rejects its\npublic receipt immediately.\n\n## Law, histories, and observation cuts\n\nThe independent two-call rule starts the successor only after both its timer\nedge and the first handler's release. The oracle enumerates 24 successful\nhold/release histories across debounce and throttle. It crosses leading,\nnon-leading, and omitted-leading options, with release before, at, and after\nthe edge. The model computes `max(eligibleAt, releaseAt)` without importing\nthe production scheduler. The immediate handoff is the chosen pacing policy;\nthe guide's serialization promise alone supplies the lower bound.\n\nThe production driver uses real Collections, strategies, and transactions.\nIt controls handler promises and a virtual clock. At admission, the timer\nedge, handler release, and final drain, it checks every handler start, active\nhandler count, transaction state, receipt outcome and identity, and optimistic\nrows. Adjacent histories distinguish a renewed debounce quiet period, throttle\nspacing from actual starts, and dropped-call admission isolation. The\nsettlement grammar crosses both factories, leading modes, same/distinct keys,\nfirst-handler success or rejection, and pending cancellation. Manual rollback\ncrosses debounce, throttle, and queue. Nested `onMutate` checks one merged\nleading write, no stale timer, and a later usable admission.\n\nOriginal production fails 18 of the 24 held-edge histories at the concurrency\nassertion. The six release-before-edge controls pass. Before the final repair,\nmanual rollback fails at the active-handler assertion for each of the three\nstrategies. Nested admission fails for debounce and throttle. These are\nassertion kills at the promised intermediate cuts, not timeouts. The repaired\nowner and ordinary pacing tests pass 136/136 on the reviewed base. Focused\nTypeScript, ESLint, Prettier, and the DB package build pass. The build reports\nnon-fatal missing Expo example configuration warnings.\n\n## Oracle guide audit\n\n| Requirement | Result for this owner |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The guide supplies serialization and timing authority. The opening oracle prose and coverage map state finite limits. |\n| ORC-002 | Expected times come from timer eligibility and handler release, not a production pacer or classifier. |\n| ORC-003 | Opening prose states the contract; nearby model, action grammar, production driver, and assertions remain distinct. |\n| ORC-004 | The 24-cell matrix is bounded enumeration with explicit release offsets. It does not claim random reach. |\n| ORC-005 | Real production entry points reach held handlers. The recorder retains ordered starts and intermediate receipts. |\n| ORC-006 | Original overlap, early release after rollback, and duplicate leading starts fail their intended assertions. |\n| ORC-007 | No important random property is introduced. Named finite tests provide direct replay. |\n| ORC-008 | The two-call expected rule is a prerequisite calculation, not a copied production state machine. |\n| ORC-009 | Model `pendingIds` combines optimistic mutations. Handler completion and public receipt settlement stay separate. |\n| ORC-010 | Cleanup releases held promises and preserves a primary assertion when cleanup also fails. |\n| ORC-011 | A second equivalent formulation is unnecessary for the max-prerequisite rule. The issue's direct two-call probe provides a separate public concurrency observation. |\n| ORC-012 | This record states the bounded closure, kills, checks, limits, and each guide outcome for the reviewed product commit. |\n| ORC-013 | Edge-before/at/after releases, renewed quiet, delayed actual-start spacing, and nested admission reject nearby wrong designs. |\n| ORC-014 | The claim is limited to controlled DB source execution. No real-server, framework, or npm handoff is claimed. |\n\nThe repaired source paths have no known reachable counterexample in the named\ngrammar. The coverage map owns provider echo, cleanup during a held handler,\nlonger arbitrary bursts, held default options, and multiple-manager histories.\nThose gaps limit the evidence; a passing finite suite does not prove every\npossible history. Server ordering, framework scheduling, and published npm\nbehavior require separate receiving witnesses.\n"}
